.he "wssize (1)                   8/11/26                   wssize (1)"
.fo "				    -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
PROGRAM
.in +5
.br
.bd
wssize
- print the segment sizes of an object file


.in -5
.bd
USAGE
.in +5
.br
.bd
wssize file ...


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Wssize
prints the size of each segment of an object file or an executable, and
their total.  One line per file, under a heading:

.in +5
.sp
   text	   data	    bss	    dec	    hex	filename
.sp
.in -5
.bd
Text
is the code,
.bd
data
the initialised data, and
.bd
bss
the data that is zero when the program starts and so occupies no room
in the file.  The total is given twice, in decimal and in hexadecimal,
because one is easier to compare against the last build and the other
against an address.

An archive is read member by member and each member is reported under
its own name, so the size of a library is the sum of what it would
contribute rather than the size of the file.

The number that matters on this machine is the total.  A program has
to fit in memory alongside whatever it allocates, and bss counts
against that even though it costs nothing on disc.

There are no options.

.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ccc (1), wsld (1), wsnm (1), wslib (1)


.in -5
.bd
DIAGNOSTICS
.in +5
.br
A file too small to hold a header, or one whose header is not in the
expected format, is reported and skipped; the remaining files are
still read.
